sage data via read(0,buf,size), stores pointer in msg[cookie_num] and size in msg_size[cookie_num], increments cookie_num.\"},{\"name\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14e1\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"_abort\",\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"random_byte\",\"source\":\"fread from /dev/urandom\",\"type\":\"char\",\"stack\":\"rbp-0xc\"}],\"checks\":[\"if cookie_num == 0 → _abort('No cookie!')\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"fread(&random_byte, 1, 1, urandom)\"},{\"operation\":\"COMPUTE\",\"description\":\"index = random_byte % cookie_num (signed idiv)\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + index*8\",\"destination\":\"printf argument\"},{\"operation\":\"FREE\",\"target\":\"msg[index]\",\"note\":\"free(msg[index]) — pointer NOT nulled after free\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + (cookie_num-1)*8\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + index*8\",\"note\":\"swap-remove: last element moved to freed slot\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + (cookie_num-1)*4\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + index*4\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num -= 1\",\"address\":\"0x41e8\"}],\"calls\":[\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"Eats (deletes) a random cookie: reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, computes index = byte % cookie_num, prints msg[index] via printf(%s), frees msg[index], then swap-removes by moving msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[index] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[index], decrements cookie_num. The freed pointer is NOT explicitly nulled — if index == cookie_num-1, the swap writes the same freed pointer back into msg[index].\"},{\"name\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1628\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\",\"stack\":\"rbp-0x10\"},{\"name\":\"edit_data\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"type\":\"raw bytes\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed less-than comparison, but index read as unsigned %llu)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[index]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + index*4\",\"destination\":\"read size = msg_size[index] - 1\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + index*8\",\"destination\":\"read buffer pointer\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index] buffer\",\"size\":\"msg_size[index] - 1 (computed as int32, sign-extended via movsxd)\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index][read_return_value] = 0x00 if last byte == 0x0a\",\"note\":\"newline replacement\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"Edits a cookie message: reads index via scanf(%llu), checks index < cookie_num (signed jl), then reads msg_size[index]-1 bytes into msg[index] buffer via read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). The size is computed as msg_size[index] (int32) - 1, sign-extended to int64 via movsxd. If msg_size[index] is 0, the subtraction yields -1 which sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFF, causing read to attempt a massive write.\"},{\"name\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1751\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\",\"stack\":\"rbp-0x10\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed less-than comparison, but index read as unsigned %llu)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + index*8\",\"destination\":\"printf(%s) argument\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\"],\"summary\":\"Views a cookie message: reads index via scanf(%llu), checks index < cookie_num (signed jl), then prints msg[index] as a string via printf('%s', msg[index]).\"},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts\"],\"summary\":\"Prints the menu options via puts.\"},{\"name\":\"main\",\"address\":\"0x187f\",\"callers\":[\"__libc_start_main\"],\"callees\":[\"init\",\"menu\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"eat_cookie\",\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"choice\",\"source\":\"scanf(%lld)\",\"type\":\"int64\",\"stack\":\"rbp-0x10\"}],\"checks\":[\"choice == 1 → eat_cookie\",\"choice == 2 → create_cookie\",\"choice == 3 → edit_cookie\",\"choice == 4 → read_cookie\",\"choice == 5 → exit(0)\"],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"init\",\"menu\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"eat_cookie\",\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\",\"exit\"],\"summary\":\"Main loop: calls init(), then loops: print menu, scanf(%lld) choice, dispatch to eat/create/edit/read/exit.\"}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_byte from /dev/urandom (not attacker-controlled, but determines which slot is freed)\",\"operation\":\"FREE msg[index] then swap-remove without nulling freed pointer\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"After free(msg[index]), if index == cookie_num-1, the swap writes the same freed pointer back into msg[index], leaving a dangling pointer. Even if index != cookie_num-1, the freed chunk may be reallocated by subsequent create_cookie or strdup, and the old pointer in msg[cookie_num-1] (now moved to msg[index]) still points to valid memory — but the freed chunk itself can be reused. The freed pointer is never nulled, creating potential UAF if the freed chunk is reallocated and then accessed via edit/read.\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu) and edit_data via read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE into msg[index] buffer with size = msg_size[index] - 1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"The read size is computed as msg_size[index] (int32) - 1, then sign-extended via movsxd. If msg_size[index] == 0, the result is -1 → 0xFFFFFFFF (sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), causing read() to attempt writing a massive amount into the heap buffer. This is a potential heap overflow. Additionally, if msg[index] points to freed memory (UAF from eat_cookie), this write goes into a freed chunk.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[index] and print via printf(%s)\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"If msg[index] points to freed/reallocated heap memory (UAF from eat_cookie), printf(%s) will print whatever is in that memory, potentially leaking heap metadata or other data. This is a potential info leak.\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu)\",\"operation\":\"index check uses signed jl but index is read as unsigned %llu\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"The index is read via scanf(%llu) (unsigned) but the bounds check uses 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed less-than). If cookie_num is small and index is a large unsigned value with bit 63 set, the signed comparison may pass, allowing OOB access to msg[] and msg_size[] arrays. However, this requires cookie_num to be positive and the index to have bit 63 set, which is an edge case.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu)\",\"operation\":\"same signed/unsigned mismatch in bounds check\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"Same signed jl comparison on unsigned %llu input as edit_cookie — potential OOB read of msg[] array.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"size via scanf(%lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(size, 1) then read(0, buf, size)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"Size is checked <= 0x100 and calloc zeroes memory. The read size equals the allocation size, so no overflow here. The size is stored as int32 in msg_size[].\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is a 64-bit PIE ELF, dynamically linked, not stripped, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Security: Full RELRO, NX enabled, Canary, PIE enabled, no FORTIFY\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg (0x4060, 32×8=256 bytes, char* array), msg_size (0x4160, 32×4=128 bytes, int32 array), urandom (0x41e0, 8 bytes, FILE*), cookie_num (0x41e8, 8 bytes, int64). msg and msg_size are parallel arrays indexed by the same cookie index.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-creates 5 fortune cookies via strdup into msg[0..4] with sizes 0x50,0x30,0x18,0x38,0x40 in msg_size[0..4], sets cookie_num=5\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie: scanf(%lld) for size, checks size<=0x100, calloc(size,1), read(0,buf,size), stores in msg[cookie_num] and msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++. Check cookie_num<=0x1f (max 32 slots).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie: reads 1 random byte from /dev/urandom, index=byte%cookie_num, prints msg[index] via printf(%s), frees msg[index], swap-removes msg[cookie_num-1]→msg[index] and msg_size[cookie_num-1]→msg_size[index], cookie_num--. Freed pointer is NOT nulled.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(%llu) for index, checks index<cookie_num via signed jl, reads msg_size[index]-1 bytes into msg[index] via read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). Size computed as int32-1 then sign-extended via movsxd.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie: scanf(%llu) for index, checks index<cookie_num via signed jl, prints msg[index] via printf(%s).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, when index == cookie_num-1 (the last element), the swap-remove writes msg[cookie_num-1] back into msg[index] (same slot), so msg[index] still contains the freed pointer — a dangling pointer remains in the msg[] array.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, if msg_size[index] == 0, the expression msg_size[index]-1 yields -1 (int32), which is sign-extended via movsxd to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, causing read() to attempt writing ~4 exabytes into the heap buffer at msg[index].\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie both use scanf(%llu) for index but check bounds with 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed less-than). If index has bit 63 set, the signed comparison treats it as negative, potentially passing the check and allowing OOB array access.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is read-only (Full RELRO) — GOT overwrite is not viable. All GOT entries are in the RELRO-protected region.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is set in init(), limiting runtime to 60 seconds.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"The index in eat_cookie is determined by a random byte from /dev/urandom modulo cookie_num — the attacker does NOT directly control which cookie is freed.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size entries are stored as int32 (DWORD) but cookie_num and msg entries are int64/pointer (QWORD). create_cookie stores size via 'mov ecx,edx' (truncates to 32-bit) into msg_size[].\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"The 5 initial strdup allocations in init() create heap chunks of sizes 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40 (plus strdup overhead). These are in the same heap arena as subsequent create_cookie calloc calls.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 256 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 128 bytes) are contiguous in BSS with no gap between them (0x4060+256=0x4160). urandom (0x41e0) follows msg_size (0x4160+128=0x41e0). cookie_num (0x41e8) follows urandom.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the signed/unsigned mismatch in edit_cookie/read_cookie bounds check is practically exploitable — requires index with bit 63 set via scanf(%llu), and the behavior of jl on such values needs runtime verification.\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis shows the mismatch, but actual exploitability depends on runtime behavior of scanf(%llu) with large values and the signed comparison.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size[index] can be set to 0 by an attacker — create_cookie stores the user-provided size (which must be >0 for calloc to return non-NULL), but the swap-remove in eat_cookie could potentially leave a slot with msg_size=0 if the last element's size is 0. Need to verify if any code path can produce msg_size[i]=0.\",\"reason\":\"The init() pre-set sizes are all non-zero. create_cookie checks size<=0x100 but does not check size>0. If size=0, calloc(0,1) may return NULL or a minimal allocation, and msg_size would be 0.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie UAF (dangling pointer when index==cookie_num-1) can be reliably triggered — the index is random (byte % cookie_num), so the attacker cannot directly control which slot is freed.\",\"reason\":\"The randomness comes from /dev/urandom, which is not predictable. However, by manipulating cookie_num (creating/deleting cookies), the attacker can influence the modulo result.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"CET (IBT/SHSTK) status is unknown — checksec does not report it and the ELF may or may not have CET-related notes.\",\"reason\":\"No CET information available from standard tools.\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether calloc(0, 1) in create_cookie returns NULL or a valid minimal allocation on glibc 2.39 — if it returns NULL, msg[cookie_num] would be NULL and subsequent edit/read would crash.\",\"reason\":\"Implementation-defined behavior of calloc(0,1) needs runtime verification.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB pie executable, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, BuildID=84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2, for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, GCC 10.2.0 (Debian)\",\"sections\":\"29 sections. Key: .text@0x1110(0x891,AX), .rodata@0x2000(0x266,A), .data@0x4000(0x10,WA), .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,WA), .got@0x3f58(0xa8,WA→RELRO), .plt@0x1020(0xe0,AX), .init_array@0x3d58(0x8,WA→RELRO), .fini_array@0x3d60(0x8,WA→RELRO), .dynamic@0x3d68(0x1f0,WA→RELRO)\",\"symbols\":\"Key user functions: _abort@0x11f5, init@0x1232, create_cookie@0x1399, eat_cookie@0x14e1, edit_cookie@0x1628, read_cookie@0x1751, menu@0x17fd, main@0x187f. Key globals: msg@0x4060(256B,char*[32]), msg_size@0x4160(128B,int32[32]), urandom@0x41e0(8B,FILE*), cookie_num@0x41e8(8B,int64)\",\"disassembly\":\"Full disassembly of all 8 user functions obtained. Key data flows: eat_cookie frees without nulling (UAF), edit_cookie uses msg_size[idx]-1 as read size (potential integer underflow to 0xFFFFFFFF when msg_size=0), read_cookie prints msg[idx] as string (potential info leak), edit/read_cookie use signed jl on unsigned %llu index (potential OOB).\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":3,\"medium_severity\":0,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, free(msg[index]) is called but the freed pointer is never set to NULL. The subsequent swap-remove operation (msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1]) writes msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[index]. When index == cookie_num-1 (the last element), this writes the same freed pointer back into msg[index], leaving a dangling pointer in the msg[] array. Even when index != cookie_num-1, the freed chunk at the old msg[index] position is not nulled before being overwritten by the swap. The dangling pointer can later be dereferenced by edit_cookie (write) or read_cookie (read) if the freed chunk is reallocated and the slot is accessed again. The root cause is the absence of pointer invalidation after free.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[] (0x4060, char* array, 32 entries)\",\"msg_size[] (0x4160, int32 array, 32 entries)\",\"heap chunks allocated by calloc/strdup\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_byte from /dev/urandom determines which cookie slot is freed; attacker influences cookie_num via create/eat operations to control modulo result\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"fread(&random_byte, 1, 1, urandom) → random_byte\",\"index = random_byte % cookie_num (signed idiv at 0x153e)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) — prints cookie before freeing\",\"free(msg[index]) at 0x158e — pointer freed but NOT nulled\",\"msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15c6 — swap-remove; if index==cookie_num-1, freed pointer written back\",\"msg_size[index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15fc\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x160a\",\"Later: edit_cookie reads msg[index] as buffer pointer and writes to it (0x16d2, 0x16de)\",\"Later: read_cookie reads msg[index] and prints via printf(%s) (0x17ce, 0x17e1)\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After free(msg[index]), the pointer should be set to NULL or the slot should be marked invalid to prevent subsequent access to freed memory.\",\"actual\":\"No NULL assignment or invalidation occurs after free. The swap-remove overwrites msg[index] with msg[cookie_num-1], but when index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back into the same slot.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing pointer invalidation (NULL assignment) after free. The swap-remove logic does not account for the case where index == cookie_num-1, leaving a dangling pointer.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"FREE without pointer invalidation, followed by potential DEREFERENCE via edit/read\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free), 0x15c6 (swap-write of freed pointer), 0x16d2/0x16de (edit dereference), 0x17ce/0x17e1 (read dereference)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[index]); msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1]; // no NULL when index==cookie_num-1\",\"target\":\"heap chunk previously allocated by calloc/strdup\",\"size\":\"N/A (UAF — size depends on original allocation)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed chunk is reallocated (e.g., by a subsequent create_cookie calloc or strdup call) and then accessed via edit_cookie, the write operation read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) writes attacker-controlled data into the reallocated chunk, potentially corrupting heap metadata or overlapping heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed chunk is reallocated and then accessed via read_cookie, printf('%s', msg[index]) prints the contents of the reallocated chunk, which may contain heap metadata, pointers, or other sensitive data from the new allocation.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed chunk is not reallocated and the dangling pointer is dereferenced, the memory may have been returned to the allocator, causing a segfault on access.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[index]) is called at 0x158e. The pointer in msg[index] is freed but no subsequent instruction sets msg[index] = NULL.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15b0-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Swap-remove: msg[cookie_num-1] is loaded and written into msg[index]. When index == cookie_num-1, this writes the freed pointer back into the same slot, creating a dangling pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is decremented but msg[index] is not nulled. No NULL assignment instruction exists between free (0x158e) and ret (0x1627).\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2-0x16de\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie loads msg[index] as the buffer pointer and calls read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). If msg[index] is a dangling pointer from eat_cookie, this writes to freed/reallocated memory.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17ce-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie loads msg[index] and passes it to printf('%s', msg[index]). If msg[index] is a dangling pointer, this reads freed/reallocated memory.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that the freed chunk can be reallocated by a subsequent create_cookie or strdup call before the dangling pointer is accessed\",\"Verify the probability of index == cookie_num-1 given the random byte modulo cookie_num\",\"Confirm that edit_cookie or read_cookie can access the slot containing the dangling pointer after eat_cookie\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_SIZE_COMPUTATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the read size is computed as msg_size[index] - 1. msg_size[index] is a 32-bit signed integer (loaded as DWORD at 0x16b6). The subtraction 'sub eax, 0x1' at 0x16b9 produces -1 (0xFFFFFFFF) when msg_size[index] == 0. This 32-bit result is then sign-extended to 64 bits via 'movsxd rdx, eax' at 0x16bc, yielding 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This value is passed as the size argument to read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de, causing read() to attempt writing approximately 18 exabytes into the heap buffer at msg[index]. The root cause is the absence of a check for msg_size[index] == 0 before computing the read size, combined with signed arithmetic on a value that can be zero.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size[] (0x4160, int32 array, 32 entries)\",\"heap buffer at msg[index]\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_size via scanf(%lld) in create_cookie (size=0 is allowed); index via scanf(%llu) in edit_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie → edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker calls create_cookie with size=0\",\"create_cookie: scanf(%lld) reads size=0 at 0x13ed\",\"create_cookie: cmp rax, 0x100; jle — size=0 passes the <=0x100 check at 0x13f6\",\"create_cookie: calloc(0, 1) at 0x1416 — returns NULL or minimal allocation\",\"create_cookie: msg_size[cookie_num] = 0 (via mov ecx, edx at 0x1498, truncating 0 to int32 0)\",\"Attacker calls edit_cookie with the index of the size-0 cookie\",\"edit_cookie: scanf(%llu) reads index at 0x1671\",\"edit_cookie: loads msg_size[index] as DWORD → 0 at 0x16b6\",\"edit_cookie: sub eax, 0x1 → eax = -1 (0xFFFFFFFF) at 0x16b9\",\"edit_cookie: movsxd rdx, eax → rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF at 0x16bc\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de — massive heap overflow\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Before computing msg_size[index] - 1, the code should verify that msg_size[index] > 0 to prevent underflow. Additionally, create_cookie should reject size=0.\",\"actual\":\"create_cookie only checks size <= 0x100 (at 0x13f6) but does not check size > 0. edit_cookie computes msg_size[index] - 1 without checking if msg_size[index] is 0, and the result is sign-extended to 64 bits.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check on msg_size[index] before subtraction. Missing size > 0 check in create_cookie.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"WRITE via read() with underflowed size\",\"address\":\"0x16b6 (load msg_size), 0x16b9 (sub 1), 0x16bc (movsxd), 0x16de (read call)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], (int64)(int32)(msg_size[index] - 1)) where msg_size[index]==0 → read(0, buf, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"target\":\"heap buffer at msg[index]\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (sign-extended -1)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read() with size 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF will attempt to write far beyond the allocated heap buffer, corrupting adjacent heap chunks, heap metadata, and potentially other heap objects. In practice, read() will write until it hits an unmapped page, causing massive heap corruption before crashing.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The massive write will eventually reach unmapped memory and trigger SIGSEGV, terminating the process.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6\",\"description\":\"Size check: 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle 140a' — only checks size <= 0x100, does NOT check size > 0. size=0 passes this check.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1498\",\"description\":\"msg_size[cookie_num] = size via 'mov ecx, edx' — truncates to int32. When size=0, msg_size[cookie_num] = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] loaded as DWORD (int32). If the value is 0, the subsequent subtraction produces -1.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b9-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"'sub eax, 0x1' (0 → -1 = 0xFFFFFFFF) followed by 'movsxd rdx, eax' sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[index], rdx) where rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF — read() is called with a massive size, causing heap buffer overflow.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that calloc(0, 1) returns a valid pointer (not NULL) on glibc 2.39, so that msg[index] is non-NULL and read() proceeds\",\"Verify that the edit_cookie index check passes for the slot where msg_size was set to 0\",\"Confirm the actual behavior of read() with size 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (likely returns EFAULT or writes until SIGSEGV)\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"In both edit_cookie and read_cookie, the cookie index is read via scanf('%llu') as an unsigned 64-bit integer, but the bounds check uses 'cmp rdx, rax; jl' (signed less-than jump). The jl instruction performs a signed comparison. If the attacker provides an index with bit 63 set (e.g., 0x8000000000000000 or larger), the signed comparison treats it as a negative number, which is less than the positive cookie_num, so the check passes. This allows out-of-bounds access to the msg[] array (at 0x4060, 32 entries of 8 bytes) and msg_size[] array (at 0x4160, 32 entries of 4 bytes). An index with bit 63 set causes the array access 'msg[index]' to compute an address far outside the 32-element array bounds, reading/writing arbitrary memory relative to the msg[] base address.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[] (0x4060, char* array, 32 entries, 256 bytes)\",\"msg_size[] (0x4160, int32 array, 32 entries, 128 bytes)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu) in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie, read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"scanf('%llu', &index) reads unsigned 64-bit index at 0x1671 (edit) / 0x179a (read)\",\"index loaded into rdx at 0x1676 (edit) / 0x179f (read)\",\"cookie_num loaded into rax at 0x167a (edit) / 0x17a3 (read)\",\"cmp rdx, rax; jl — signed comparison at 0x1681/0x1684 (edit) / 0x17aa/0x17ad (read)\",\"If index has bit 63 set: signed comparison treats index as negative → passes check\",\"msg[index] accessed at 0x16c3-0x16d2 (edit) / 0x17bf-0x17ce (read) — OOB array access\",\"edit: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) — writes to OOB pointer\",\"read: printf('%s', msg[index]) — reads from OOB pointer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The bounds check should use unsigned comparison (jb/jae) since the index is read as unsigned %llu. The check should verify that the unsigned index is less than cookie_num.\",\"actual\":\"The check uses 'jl' (signed less-than), which treats large unsigned values with bit 63 set as negative, incorrectly passing the bounds check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Signed comparison (jl) used instead of unsigned comparison (jb) for an unsigned input value.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access via signedness mismatch\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684 (edit check), 0x16c3-0x16d2 (edit access), 0x17aa-0x17ad (read check), 0x17bf-0x17ce (read access)\",\"expression\":\"if ((signed)index < cookie_num) { access msg[index]; } — signed comparison on unsigned input\",\"target\":\"msg[] array at 0x4060 and msg_size[] array at 0x4160\",\"size\":\"8 bytes per msg[] entry, 4 bytes per msg_size[] entry\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, if the signedness bypass is triggered, msg[index] is read from an out-of-bounds location relative to the msg[] array base (0x4060). The value at that OOB location is treated as a pointer and passed to printf('%s'), which dereferences it and prints the string at that address. This can leak memory contents from BSS or other sections.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, if the signedness bypass is triggered, msg[index] is read from an OOB location and used as the buffer pointer for read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). Additionally, msg_size[index] is read from an OOB location in the msg_size[] array. The write destination is whatever pointer value exists at the OOB location, and the write size is derived from the OOB msg_size value.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The OOB read in read_cookie can expose memory contents from beyond the msg[] array, including pointers stored in BSS (e.g., urandom FILE* at 0x41e0, cookie_num at 0x41e8) or other data.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"Index read via scanf('%llu') — unsigned 64-bit format specifier. The index is stored as uint64 on the stack at [rbp-0x10].\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 1692' — jl is a signed less-than jump. If index (rdx) has bit 63 set, it is treated as negative and passes the check against positive cookie_num (rax).\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a\",\"description\":\"Index read via scanf('%llu') — same unsigned format specifier as edit_cookie.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 17bb' — same signed jl comparison on unsigned input. Same signedness mismatch as edit_cookie.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3/0x17bf\",\"description\":\"After the check passes, msg[index] is accessed via 'lea rdx, [rax*8+0x0]; lea rax, [rip+msg]; mov rax, [rdx+rax]' — the index is used directly as an array offset. With bit 63 set, the computed address is far outside the 32-element msg[] array, causing OOB access.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that scanf('%llu') accepts values with bit 63 set (e.g., 0x8000000000000000)\",\"Verify that the jl instruction treats such values as negative and passes the check\",\"Confirm the actual memory layout and what data is accessible at the OOB indices\",\"Determine if the OOB msg[index] value is a valid pointer that can be dereferenced without immediate crash\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"The read(0, buf, size) call in create_cookie uses the same size for both calloc and read, so there is no buffer overflow in the normal path (size > 0, size <= 0x100).\",\"reason\":\"calloc(size, 1) allocates exactly 'size' bytes, and read(0, buf, size) reads at most 'size' bytes. The allocation and read sizes match, preventing overflow. The size=0 case is covered by BUG-002 (integer underflow in edit_cookie, not in create_cookie itself).\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"The menu dispatch uses scanf('%lld') for choice and compares against 1-5. No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\",\"reason\":\"The choice is checked against specific values (1-5) with direct comparisons, not array indexing. No bounds check issue.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether calloc(0, 1) returns NULL or a valid minimal allocation on glibc 2.39\",\"reason\":\"If calloc(0,1) returns NULL, then msg[index] = NULL and the edit_cookie read() call would write to address 0x0, causing a NULL pointer dereference crash rather than a heap overflow. If it returns a valid pointer, the heap overflow in BUG-002 proceeds.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Runtime verification of calloc(0,1) behavior on the target system\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie UAF can be reliably triggered given the random index from /dev/urandom\",\"reason\":\"The index is random_byte % cookie_num. The attacker cannot directly control which slot is freed, but can influence cookie_num by creating/deleting cookies. When cookie_num == 1, index is always 0 == cookie_num-1, guaranteeing the dangling pointer case.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic analysis to confirm the UAF trigger path, especially the cookie_num==1 case\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the signed/unsigned mismatch in edit_cookie/read_cookie is practically exploitable\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis confirms the jl instruction on unsigned input, but the actual behavior with very large index values (bit 63 set) needs runtime confirmation — the array access may compute an address in unmapped memory, causing immediate crash before any useful OOB access.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic testing with large unsigned index values to confirm the bypass and determine what memory is accessible\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":2,\"verified\":1,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":1,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"In eat_cookie, free(msg[index]) is called but the freed pointer is never set to NULL. When index == cookie_num-1, the swap-remove writes the same freed pointer back into msg[index] (self-assignment), leaving a dangling pointer. After 5 consecutive eat operations, cookie_num reaches 0, and msg[0] retains a non-NULL dangling pointer to freed heap memory.\",\"description\":\"After eating all 5 initial cookies (cookie_num 5->0), the 5th eat (cookie_num=1) frees msg[0] and self-assigns msg[0]=msg[0], leaving a dangling pointer. Attempting to access msg[0] via read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 triggers the bounds check failure (index 0 >= cookie_num 0), causing _abort -> exit(1).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"free(msg[index]) without NULL assignment\",\"index == cookie_num-1 → self-assignment leaves dangling pointer\",\"cookie_num decremented to 0 → slot inaccessible\",\"read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 → bounds check fails → _abort → exit(1)\",\"CRASH\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"The ability to trigger process termination by attempting to access a slot when cookie_num=0\",\"how\":\"Eat 5 cookies to reduce cookie_num to 0, then attempt read_cookie(0) or edit_cookie(0). The bounds check (index < cookie_num = 0) fails, calling _abort which calls exit(1).\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":false},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be reduced to 0 by eating all cookies (5 eat operations from initial state)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to make the dangling slot inaccessible and trigger the bounds check failure\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"The 5th eat must occur when cookie_num=1, so index=0%1=0 and index==cookie_num-1=0, causing self-assignment that leaves the dangling pointer\",\"necessity\":\"Required to create the dangling pointer in msg[0]\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"User must attempt to access msg[0] via read_cookie or edit_cookie after cookie_num=0\",\"necessity\":\"Required to trigger the _abort/exit(1) crash\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Start the binary, eat 5 cookies (option 1 x5) to reduce cookie_num from 5 to 0. 2. Attempt read_cookie(0) (option 4, index 0). 3. Observe process termination with return_code=1 (_abort -> exit(1)).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"return_code: 1 (exit(1) from _abort)\",\"Process terminates after attempting read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0\",\"gdb confirms msg[0] = 0x555555559540 (non-NULL dangling pointer) after 5th eat\",\"gdb confirms cookie_num = 0 after 5th eat\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on this primitive — RELRO protects GOT, not relevant to UAF or bounds check\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on this primitive — NX prevents code execution, not relevant to crash\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on this primitive — Canary protects stack, not relevant to heap UAF\"},{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on this primitive — PIE randomizes base address, not relevant to crash via _abort\"},{\"name\":\"alarm(60)\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Limits runtime to 60 seconds, but crash occurs well within this limit\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"gdb: After 5th eat (cookie_num 1->0), msg[0] = 0x0000555555559540 (non-NULL dangling pointer). free(msg[0]) was called at 0x158e but msg[0] was not nulled. cookie_num = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"PoC: read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 triggers _abort (exit(1)). return_code=1. The bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' fails because index 0 is not less than cookie_num 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"free(msg[index]) at 0x158e, then msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15c6. When index == cookie_num-1, this is self-assignment, leaving the freed pointer in place.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"_abort\",\"address\":\"0x11f5\",\"description\":\"_abort calls printf then exit(1). Called when bounds check fails in read_cookie/edit_cookie.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":1,\"details\":\"PoC executed: 5 eat operations reduce cookie_num to 0, leaving dangling pointer in msg[0] (gdb: 0x555555559540). read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 triggers _abort -> exit(1), return_code=1. NOTE: This crash is a bounds check failure, NOT a UAF dereference. The dangling pointer exists but is never dereferenced — the bounds check prevents access before any dereference can occur.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"PRIM-001 is VERIFIED as a CRASH primitive, but it is important to note that this crash is NOT a UAF dereference. It is a bounds check failure (_abort -> exit(1)) that occurs because cookie_num=0 blocks access to the slot containing the dangling pointer. The dangling pointer in msg[0] is never actually dereferenced. The crash is a side effect of the UAF code path (cookie_num decremented to 0), not a direct consequence of the dangling pointer being used.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"HEAP_OBJECT_CONTROL\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"In eat_cookie, free(msg[index]) is called but the freed pointer is never set to NULL. When index == cookie_num-1, the swap-remove writes the same freed pointer back into msg[index] (self-assignment), leaving a dangling pointer in the msg[] array.\",\"description\":\"After eating cookies to reduce cookie_num, the dangling pointer in msg[index] points to freed heap memory. The freed chunk enters tcache. However, the dangling pointer is NOT directly exploitable because: (a) when index == cookie_num-1, cookie_num decrements to make the slot inaccessible; (b) when index != cookie_num-1, the swap-remove overwrites the dangling pointer with msg[cookie_num-1]; (c) calloc (used by create_cookie) does NOT reuse tcache chunks on glibc 2.39, so the freed chunk is never reallocated into the same slot.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"free(msg[index]) without NULL assignment\",\"When index == cookie_num-1: self-assignment leaves dangling pointer in msg[index]\",\"cookie_num-- makes slot inaccessible (index >= cookie_num)\",\"When index != cookie_num-1: swap-remove overwrites dangling pointer\",\"calloc does NOT reuse tcache chunk (glibc 2.39)\",\"Dangling pointer exists but cannot be dereferenced\",\"HEAP_OBJECT_CONTROL (CANDIDATE — not directly exploitable)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"A dangling pointer to freed heap memory exists in the msg[] array after eat_cookie, but it cannot be dereferenced through normal array access\",\"how\":\"Eat cookies to trigger the UAF (index == cookie_num-1 case). The dangling pointer is left in msg[index], but cookie_num decrements to make the slot inaccessible. create_cookie overwrites the slot with a new calloc allocation (different address, no tcache reuse).\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":false},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie must select index == cookie_num-1 to leave a dangling pointer (self-assignment case). This occurs when random_byte % cookie_num == cookie_num-1. When cookie_num=1, index is always 0 == cookie_num-1, guaranteeing the dangling pointer.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to create the dangling pointer in msg[]\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"The dangling pointer in msg[index] must be accessible via edit_cookie or read_cookie. This requires cookie_num > index, but eat_cookie decrements cookie_num to make index == cookie_num (inaccessible).\",\"necessity\":\"Required to dereference the dangling pointer — THIS PRECONDITION CANNOT BE SATISFIED through normal array access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The freed chunk must be reallocated into the same slot for heap object control. create_cookie uses calloc, which does NOT reuse tcache chunks on glibc 2.39.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for heap reuse — THIS PRECONDITION CANNOT BE SATISFIED because calloc skips tcache\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"Alternative: the swap-remove (index != cookie_num-1) creates a duplicate pointer in msg[cookie_num-1] (now out of bounds). This out-of-bounds slot could be accessed via BUG-003 (signedness mismatch), but that is a different BUG.\",\"necessity\":\"Alternative path requiring BUG-003 — not part of BUG-001\",\"verified\":false}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[index] after free when index == cookie_num-1\",\"alias\":\"Self-assignment: msg[index] = msg[cookie_num-1] where index == cookie_num-1, so msg[index] = msg[index]\",\"implication\":\"The freed pointer is written back into the same slot, creating a dangling pointer. However, cookie_num-- immediately makes this slot inaccessible (index >= new cookie_num).\"},{\"expression\":\"calloc(0x50, 1) vs freed tcache chunk of size 0x50\",\"alias\":\"calloc skips tcache on glibc 2.39 — allocates from arena instead\",\"implication\":\"The freed chunk is NOT reused by create_cookie. The dangling pointer, even if accessible, would point to freed memory that is never reallocated into the same slot. No heap object control through this path.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] after swap-remove (index != cookie_num-1)\",\"alias\":\"Out-of-bounds duplicate pointer — msg[old_cookie_num-1] still holds its original value after being copied to msg[index]\",\"implication\":\"The old value in msg[cookie_num-1] is a duplicate pointer to a valid chunk. This slot is now out of bounds (cookie_num decremented). Accessing it requires BUG-003 (signedness mismatch), not BUG-001.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. gdb: Set breakpoint at eat_cookie+0xb2 (after free). Eat 5 cookies. Verify msg[0] is non-NULL (dangling pointer) and cookie_num=0. 2. gdb: Set breakpoint at create_cookie+0x82 (after calloc). Verify calloc returns a different address than the freed chunk (no tcache reuse). 3. PoC: Attempt read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 — verify _abort is triggered (bounds check fails). 4. PoC: Create new cookie (cookie_num 0->1), edit/read msg[0] — verify it operates on the NEW allocation, not the freed chunk.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"gdb: msg[0] = 0x555555559540 (non-NULL dangling pointer) after 5th eat\",\"gdb: cookie_num = 0 after 5th eat (slot inaccessible)\",\"gdb: calloc returns 0x55555555a5e0 (different address, no tcache reuse)\",\"PoC: edit/read on msg[0] after create_cookie operates on NEW allocation (EDITDATA read back)\",\"PoC: read_cookie(0) with cookie_num=0 triggers _abort (rc=1)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No direct impact on UAF primitive — RELRO protects GOT, not heap pointers\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact — NX prevents code execution on stack/heap, not relevant to heap object control\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact — Canary protects stack return address, not heap pointers\"},{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on primitive existence — PIE randomizes addresses but the dangling pointer still exists\"},{\"name\":\"glibc 2.39 calloc behavior\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"CRITICAL: calloc skips tcache on glibc 2.39, preventing heap chunk reuse via create_cookie. This blocks the heap object control path — the freed chunk is never reallocated into the same slot.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"gdb: After 5th eat (cookie_num 1->0), msg[0] = 0x0000555555559540 (non-NULL dangling pointer). free(msg[0]) was called at 0x158e, then msg[0] = msg[0] (self-assignment at 0x15c6). cookie_num = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416-0x141b\",\"description\":\"gdb: calloc(0x50, 1) returns 0x000055555555a5e0, which is a DIFFERENT address than the freed chunk at 0x0000555555559540. calloc does NOT reuse the tcache freed chunk on glibc 2.39.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2/0x17ce\",\"description\":\"PoC: After create_cookie (cookie_num 0->1), edit_cookie(0) writes 'EDITDATA' to msg[0] (new calloc allocation). read_cookie(0) reads back 'EDITDATA'. This is normal operation on the NEW allocation, NOT UAF — the dangling pointer was overwritten.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x1627\",\"description\":\"Disassembly confirms: free(msg[index]) at 0x158e, swap-remove msg[index]=msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15c6, cookie_num-- at 0x160a. No NULL assignment between free and ret.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"When index == cookie_num-1, the swap-remove is self-assignment (msg[index] = msg[index]), leaving the freed pointer in place. But cookie_num-- at 0x160a makes index >= cookie_num, blocking access.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"EDITDATA (from new allocation, not freed chunk)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PoC executed successfully. gdb confirms dangling pointer exists (msg[0]=0x555555559540 after free, non-NULL). However, the dangling pointer is NOT directly exploitable: (1) cookie_num=0 blocks access to the slot (bounds check fails); (2) create_cookie overwrites msg[0] with new calloc allocation (0x55555555a5e0, different address); (3) calloc does NOT reuse tcache chunk on glibc 2.39. edit/read after create_cookie operates on the NEW allocation, not the freed chunk. The UAF code defect is confirmed but the primitive cannot be elevated beyond CANDIDATE through normal array access.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"The UAF dangling pointer is confirmed to exist (gdb evidence: msg[0] = non-NULL after free). However, it is NOT directly exploitable through normal array access because: (1) When index == cookie_num-1 (the only case that leaves a dangling pointer), cookie_num decrements to make the slot inaccessible. (2) When index != cookie_num-1, the swap-remove overwrites the dangling pointer. (3) calloc (used by create_cookie) does NOT reuse tcache chunks on glibc 2.39, so the freed chunk is never reallocated into the same slot. The primitive could potentially be elevated if combined with BUG-003 (signedness mismatch) to access out-of-bounds slots, but that is a different BUG. The swap-remove also creates duplicate pointers in out-of-bounds slots (msg[old_cookie_num-1] retains its value after being copied to msg[index]), but accessing these also requires BUG-003.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Cannot achieve ARB_WRITE from BUG-001 alone. The dangling pointer is never dereferenced through normal array access because cookie_num blocks access to the slot. calloc does not reuse tcache chunks, so no heap object control through reallocation. ARB_WRITE would require combining with BUG-003 (signedness mismatch) to access out-of-bounds slots.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Cannot achieve INFO_LEAK from BUG-001 alone. read_cookie on the dangling pointer is blocked by cookie_num=0 (bounds check fails). After create_cookie, msg[0] points to a new calloc allocation (zeroed), not the freed chunk. No leak of freed memory contents is possible through normal access.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-003\",\"candidate_type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Cannot achieve RELATIVE_WRITE from BUG-001 alone. edit_cookie on the dangling pointer is blocked by cookie_num=0 (bounds check fails). After create_cookie, msg[0] points to a new allocation, and edit_cookie writes to the new allocation (normal operation, not UAF write).\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the UAF can be made exploitable by combining with BUG-003 (signedness mismatch) to access out-of-bounds slots containing duplicate pointers from swap-remove\",\"reason\":\"The swap-remove in eat_cookie creates duplicate pointers in out-of-bounds slots (msg[old_cookie_num-1] retains its value after being copied to msg[index]). These slots could potentially be accessed via BUG-003's signedness bypass. However, this is a cross-BUG combination that belongs to S4 (Exploit Route), not S3 (single BUG primitive analysis).\",\"required_next_step\":\"S4 should analyze whether combining BUG-001 (UAF dangling pointer/duplicate pointer) with BUG-003 (OOB access via signedness mismatch) can yield an exploitable primitive.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether calloc on glibc 2.39 can be made to reuse tcache chunks under any circumstances\",\"reason\":\"calloc skips tcache by design on glibc 2.39 (it checks the chunk's mmap flag and uses arena allocation instead). This blocks the heap reuse path for BUG-001. However, if there were a malloc-based allocation path (like strdup), tcache reuse would be possible. strdup is only called in init(), not during user interaction.\",\"required_next_step\":\"No further action for BUG-001 — calloc behavior is confirmed and blocks heap reuse.\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
